# Involutions modulo the largest normal odd-order subgroup (Kourovka Notebook Problem 4.75)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 4.75 (V. P. Shunkov), marked solved in the 21st issue: let G be a
periodic group containing an involution i and suppose that the Sylow 2-subgroups of G are either
locally cyclic or generalized quaternion; does the element iO_(2')(G) of G/O_(2')(G) always lie in
its centre?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.SpecificGroups.Quaternion
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Sylow
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Torsion
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.DenseTorsionFreeGroupsKourovka1Problem63Wanted
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.GroupClassesShared
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.InvolutionOddProductCentralKourovka11Problem13Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.InvolutionCentralModOddCoreKourovka4Problem75Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 4.75 (V. P. Shunkov). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00700282 (`KOU-4.75`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Periodic is `IsMulTorsion`. The Sylow `2`-subgroups of a periodic group are its maximal
`2`-subgroups (Mathlib's `Sylow 2 G`, defined for every group); each is required to be locally
cyclic (finitely generated subgroups cyclic; `IsLocallyCyclic` of the Kourovka 1.63 entry, reused
here) or generalized quaternion (`IsGeneralizedQuaternion` of `GroupClassesShared`:
`QuaternionGroup (2 ^ n)`, `n ≥ 1`, of order `2 ^ (n + 2)`).
`O_(2')(G)` is `oddCore G` of the Kourovka 11.13 entry, reused here: the join of the normal
subgroups of `G` all of whose elements have odd order; Kinyon and Urban (§3.2) note that in a
periodic group it is the largest such normal subgroup. As in the 11.13 entry, centrality of `i
O_(2')(G)` in the factor group `G / O_(2')(G)` is written out as commuting with every `g O_(2')(G)`:
every commutator `g i g⁻¹ i⁻¹` lies in `O_(2')(G)`. The problem is in the main list of the 21st
issue, marked solved; the solution is AI-generated and was confirmed by M. Amelio.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.DenseTorsionFreeGroupsKourovka1Problem63Wanted (IsLocallyCyclic)
open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.InvolutionOddProductCentralKourovka11Problem13Wanted (oddCore)
open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GroupClassesShared (IsGeneralizedQuaternion)

/-- Kourovka 4.75 (V. P. Shunkov): if `G` is a periodic group containing an involution `i` and every
Sylow `2`-subgroup of `G` (maximal `2`-subgroup) is locally cyclic or generalized quaternion, then
the element `i O_(2')(G)` of the factor group `G / O_(2')(G)` lies in its centre: it commutes with
every `g O_(2')(G)`, that is, every commutator `g i g⁻¹ i⁻¹` lies in `O_(2')(G)` (`oddCore G`). -/
def involutionCentralModuloOddCore : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], IsMulTorsion G → ∀ i : G, orderOf i = 2 →
    (∀ P : Sylow 2 G, IsLocallyCyclic (P : Subgroup G) ∨ IsGeneralizedQuaternion (P : Subgroup G)) →
    ∀ g : G, g * i * g⁻¹ * i⁻¹ ∈ oddCore G

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 4.75 solved, "No, not always" (an
AI-generated solution confirmed by M. Amelio). Kinyon and Urban (§3.2, using Proposition 3.5 of
§3.7) take an infinite perfect group `G` of exponent `2p` with no subgroup of order four, whose
involutions form one conjugacy class and generate `G`: its Sylow `2`-subgroups have order two, and
if `i O_(2')(G)` were central, `G / O_(2')(G)` would be generated by the image of `i`, hence cyclic
and, being perfect, trivial, contradicting that the involution `i` does not lie in `O_(2')(G)`.
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 4.75 (V. P. Shunkov); resolution: M. Kinyon
and J. Urban, Results on problems from the Kourovka Notebook, preprint, version 4, 19 September
2026; the construction rests on M. Amelio's geometric quotient construction (cited there as [3],
Proposition 6.1, Theorem 6.7, Remark 6.8), §3.2: the group G of Proposition 3.5 (§3.7: for every
sufficiently large odd prime p, an infinite three-generated perfect group of exponent 2p with no
subgroup of order four whose involutions form one conjugacy class and generate G) has Sylow
2-subgroups of order two, and every involution has non-central image in G/O_(2')(G); the Kourovka
Notebook (21st issue) marks 4.75 solved: No, not always (AI solution confirmed by M. Amelio),
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/kourovka-mathematics.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted involutionCentralModuloOddCore_refuted : ¬ involutionCentralModuloOddCore

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.InvolutionCentralModOddCoreKourovka4Problem75Wanted
